{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1778527185568,"sessionID":"ses_1e785620fffee24XVyaJozkTBN","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"grep","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-bb7fff3319b145c6","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"pattern":"tendon"},"output":"Found 154 matches (showing first 100)\n/home/runner/work/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/__issue_context__.json:\n  Line 2:   \"body\": \"related #2097\\n\\n**tendon cell**\\n\\n- reclassify to 'fibroblast' and 'part of' some 'tendon' \\n- adjust the textual def accordingly\\n\\n**otic fibrocyte**\\n- reclassify from fibrocyte to mesenchymal cell (PMID: [37720106](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37720106/))\\n- add synonyms - cochlear fibrocyte (PMID: [31866825](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31866825/)) and spiral ligament fibrocyte (PMID: [33193034](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33193034/)\\n\\n**In a seperate ticket - refine otic fibrobalst and subclasses**\\n1) Currently, otic fibroblast is linked to spiral ligmanet but it encompasses both spiral ligament and spiral limbus - This definition should be relabelled to Cochlear fibrocytes (more widely used) and treated as the general term \\n2) A spiral ligament fibrocyte term should be added and all Type I-V subclasses should be added under it \\n3) More info should be added to the textual defs of the subclasses (especially functions) \\n\\nUseful refs \\n\\n- PMID: 24727668\\n- PMID: [31866825](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31866825/)\\n- PMID: [37720106](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37720106/) - newer paper\\n- PMID: [33193034](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33193034/) - newer paper -specific to Spiral ligament fibrocytes\\n\",\n\n  Line 4:   \"title\": \"remove tendon cell and otic fibrocyte from under fibrocyte\",\n\n\n/home/runner/work/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/__pr_result__/src/patterns/data/matches/cellPartOfAnatomicalEntity.tsv:\n  Line 479: http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0000388\ttendon cell\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/UBERON_0000043\ttendon\thttp://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0000135\tfibrocyte\r\n\n\n/home/runner/work/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/__pr_result__/src/ontology/subsets/kidney_upper_slim.owl:\n  Line 46971:         <obo:IAO_0000115>A thin, loose vascular connective tissue that makes up the membranes surrounding joints and the sheaths protecting tendons (particularly flexor tendons in the hands and feet) where they pass over bony prominences. Synovial tissue contains synovial cells, which secrete a viscous liquid called synovial fluid; this liquid contains protein and hyaluronic acid and serves as a lubricant and nutrient for the joint cartilage surfaces[BTO]. Synovial tissue can be found in tendons (tissues that connect muscle to bone), bursae (fluid-filled, cushioning sacs found in spaces between tendons, ligaments, and bones), and the cavity (hollow enclosed area) that separates the bones of a freely movable joint, such as the knee or elbow[BTO].</obo:IAO_0000115>\n\n  Line 46997:         <owl:annotatedTarget>A thin, loose vascular connective tissue that makes up the membranes surrounding joints and the sheaths protecting tendons (particularly flexor tendons in the hands and feet) where they pass over bony prominences. Synovial tissue contains synovial cells, which secrete a viscous liquid called synovial fluid; this liquid contains protein and hyaluronic acid and serves as a lubricant and nutrient for the joint cartilage surfaces[BTO]. Synovial tissue can be found in tendons (tissues that connect muscle to bone), bursae (fluid-filled, cushioning sacs found in spaces between tendons, ligaments, and bones), and the cavity (hollow enclosed area) that separates the bones of a freely movable joint, such as the knee or elbow[BTO].</owl:annotatedTarget>\n\n  Line 49445:         <obo:IAO_0000115>Dense connective tissue is mainly composed of collagen type I. Crowded between the collagen fibers are rows of fibroblasts, fiber-forming cells, that manufacture the fibers. Dense connective tissue forms strong, rope-like structures such as tendons and ligaments. Tendons attach skeletal muscles to bones; ligaments connect bones to bones at joints. Ligaments are more stretchy and contain more elastic fibers than tendons. Dense connective tissue also make up the lower layers of the skin (dermis), where it is arranged in sheets.</obo:IAO_0000115>\n\n  Line 49459:         <owl:annotatedTarget>Dense connective tissue is mainly composed of collagen type I. Crowded between the collagen fibers are rows of fibroblasts, fiber-forming cells, that manufacture the fibers. Dense connective tissue forms strong, rope-like structures such as tendons and ligaments. Tendons attach skeletal muscles to bones; ligaments connect bones to bones at joints. Ligaments are more stretchy and contain more elastic fibers than tendons. Dense connective tissue also make up the lower layers of the skin (dermis), where it is arranged in sheets.</owl:annotatedTarget>\n\n\n/home/runner/work/eval-ont-agent-cl/eval-ont-agent-cl/__pr_result__/src/ontology/subsets/kidney_upper_slim.obo:\n  Line 16124: def: \"A thin, loose vascular connective tissue that makes up the membranes surrounding joints and the sheaths protecting tendons (particularly flexor tendons in the hands and feet) where they pass over bony prominences. Synovial tissue contains synovial cells, which secrete a viscous liquid called synovial fluid; this liquid contains protein and hyaluronic acid and serves as a lubricant and nutrient for the joint cartilage surfaces[BTO]. Synovial tissue can be found in tendons (tissues that connect muscle to bone), bursae (fluid-filled, cushioning sacs found in spaces between tendons, ligaments, and bones), and the cavity (hollow enclosed area) that separates the bones of a freely movable joint, such as the knee or elbow[BTO].\" [BTO:0001338, http://www.britannica.com/EBchecked/topic/578563/synovial-tissue]\n\n  Line 16894: def: \"Dense connective tissue is mainly composed of collagen type I. Crowded bet